# 🧭 AI Work & the Shape of Work-to-Come
**Saturday, 12 September 2026** · *Community brief — constructive lens*

---

## 🎥 What Creators Are Saying (YouTube)

**"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent"** — Nate Herk | AI Automation (14 hours ago · 30K views)
https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AI+agents+new+roles+workforce&sp=CAI%3D
Herk and Mark Kashef walk through how to evaluate and swap models within agent harnesses, why your skills should be portable across systems, and how to keep your "AI OS" current as models outgrow your setup. The architecture-of-work parallel: this is Builder + Orchestrator hygiene — the operational muscle that keeps a person from being locked to one tool.
💡 Constructive read: The person who knows how to pick and switch agents rather than defaulting to one is the one whose leverage grows as the tools do. Agent literacy is fast becoming the baseline maintenance skill.

**Matthew Berman — Overnight AI Workflow (reported Sept 2026)**
https://www.youtube.com/@matthew_berman/videos
Berman runs an OpenClaw-based agent stack that researches video topics, generates thumbnails, and delivers talking points to a Slack thread by morning — he wakes up to a completed production brief. Matt Wolfe and Joe Fier broke this down on The Next Wave, noting the uncomfortable economics (AI agents cost more than humans right now) and why that gap is closing fast.
💡 Constructive read: The Conductor archetype in the wild. Berman isn't a creator who uses AI — he's an operator who directs an overnight team and reviews the output. The workflow is already the product.

**Lane note:** The YouTube search (sorted by upload date) for "AI future of work jobs" returned mostly content 6–10 months old. The freshest material for this thread is coming from practitioner channels (Herk, Berman) rather than commentary channels right now.

