## 🧑‍💻 Practitioner Ground Truth (Reddit)

**r/singularity hot today** (3.97M subscribers):

- **"As so it begins…"** (3,032 ups · Discussion) — A viral tweet (3M+ views) about job displacement getting heavy engagement. The comments are reportedly split between anxiety and "we've been saying this for years." The *volume* of the signal (3M views on the original) is the data point — the displacement anxiety is very present in the general public even as the CEO class walks it back.

- **"Astra is now a certified human"** (808 ups) — Astra passing a human verification test in a game. The community reads this as capability signal, not job threat — but the framing of an AI being "certified human" is doing quiet work on the displacement intuition.

- **"Our greatest minds are finally moving on to better things"** (2,174 ups · Shitposting) — Likely humor about researchers redirecting from [field] to AI. Signals that the professional mobility narrative is in mainstream meme circulation.

**r/singularity summary:** Today's hot threads are capability-focused (Astra, OpenAI math feud, ChatGPT Images 2.5), not roles-focused. The job-anxiety content surfaces through virality rather than sustained discussion threads — which may itself be a signal that the anxiety is diffuse and crowd-level rather than being processed.

**Lane note:** r/ExperiencedDevs and r/cscareerquestions not checked directly; web signal suggests the "junior developer roles disappearing because AI handles CRUD/debugging" narrative is settled in those communities — the current debate is about what senior roles look like, not whether junior roles are shrinking.

