## 🎙️ Podcasts & Newsletters

**AI Daily Brief / NLW — "The Job Positions of the AI Future"** (July 5, 2026, Spotify)
https://open.spotify.com/episode/5vgmT6wyLVFbsWRqaqFdbo
The anchor episode for this thread: NLW lays out the full archetype map — Prototyper, Builder, Sweeper, Maintainer, Scout, Editor, Grower, Evangelist, Orchestrator, Conductor, Risk Steward — built on Boris Cherny's Claude Code role archetypes. This is the framework lens for this brief; the episode predates the Hugging Face incident and reads differently in its light.

**AI Daily Brief / NLW — "The New Jobs AI Will Create"** (May 10, 2026, Spotify)
https://open.spotify.com/episode/0GljfKbZ6jMVUKtKr9kRbs
First-principles case for why better AI does not simply mean less human work: cheaper services, broader access, continuous support, personalization, and human-trust requirements all expand total demand for labor. The constructive frame in long-form.

**NLW — "NLW on the Future of AI Agents"** (Spotify)
https://open.spotify.com/episode/0B5chQmS82NYh9caSJ1Iqs
Specific episode on agent futures; title/date not confirmed. In the orbit of the archetype work.

**Ethan Mollick — "Agency and Agents"** (One Useful Thing, Aug 31, 2026)
https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/agency-and-agents
The week's most important newsletter piece for this thread. Central question: whoever holds agency — human or AI — shapes what happens next. Mollick proposes the **Twilight Factory**: agents do most of the work, a facilitator agent decides when to loop in humans, making both better. The Hugging Face incident (700 agents, no escalation path) is his counter-example. The implied new human role: whoever designs and governs the facilitator.

---

## 🆕 New Roles & Archetypes Watch

**AI Orchestrator / Agent Orchestrator** — this is the breakout title of September 2026. Multiple institutional sources landing this week:
- ServiceNow names it the single most important AI job: the architect of people and machines, redesigning how work moves across fragmented workflows
  (https://www.servicenow.com/workflow/ai/ai-orchestrator-most-important-ai-job.html)
- BCG: the COO role is becoming an AI orchestrator enterprise-wide
  (https://www.bcg.com/publications/2026/coo-role-becomes-ai-orchestrator)
- Arion Research: "The Agent Orchestrator: The New Middle Manager Role of 2026"
  (https://www.arionresearch.com/blog/nfkxv53ktwxqkwtxwm2d03woml1c65)
- eightfold.ai: "The most important job of 2026 is the AI agent orchestration specialist"
  (https://eightfold.ai/blog/most-important-job-2026/)

Architecture-of-work mapping: **Orchestrator + Conductor** archetype is now mainstream corporate vocabulary, not just practitioner shorthand. The titles are slightly different across industries but the job description is converging.

**Facilitator Agent** (Mollick's concept, not a human role but a structural one) — implies a new human role upstream: whoever scopes, designs, and governs the facilitator. In the archetype map, this is the **Risk Steward** function fused with **Conductor**. No one has named this cleanly yet; there's a piece here.

**Data Centre Technician** — one of the most concrete physical new roles: ~2,800 new facilities under construction globally, with demand for hands-on operational staff.
(https://www.mercor.com/resources/experts/new-artificial-intelligence-job-opportunities/)

**AI SEO Specialist** — emerging fast as AI search traffic surges (one source cites 527% in a year); the role is less about keyword stuffing and more about being findable in AI-generated answers, which is a different craft.

**Human-Agent Interaction Designer** — appearing in early job postings. The UX designer for agent workflows, not interfaces.

