## ⚖️ The Debate (kept honest, kept brief)

Altman says he was "pretty wrong" about AI's near-term economic impact, walking back his June 2025 warnings about entry-level jobs. Amodei's 50% white-collar displacement claim hasn't materialized in BLS data (through March 2026, no significant difference in unemployment rates for high-AI-exposure occupations), and he has publicly softened the framing — but told Axios in September that "the first step towards solving these problems is kind of being honest with the population that these problems exist." Tech layoffs through May 2026 have passed 115,000 (Meta, Amazon, Snap all citing AI). The net WEF number is 170M created, 92M displaced by 2030 — net positive but the distribution problem (different people, different geographies) remains the unresolved policy question.

Calibration: the aggregate data doesn't yet show a displacement signal, but the CEO walkbacks and the 3M-view viral tweet about job loss happening simultaneously are themselves a signal — of how uneven the lived experience is sector by sector.

---

## 📌 Today's Signal

The week's most useful move, from multiple angles at once: the community is converging on a design question rather than an anxiety question. Mollick's Twilight Factory, the institutional pile-on around AI Orchestrator, and Nate Herk's practitioner guide to agent selection are all addressing the same underlying problem — not "will AI take my job" but "how do I design the handoff?" That is exactly the Conductor/Orchestrator archetype in action. The person who learns to ask "when should my agent ask me?" is the person building leverage. The person still asking "will AI take my job?" is one step behind the actual race, which is already about workflow architecture, not job survival.

